<p class="sub">A kind is one of the ten forms a declaration of Prosa takes in a <code>.proz</code> file. The kinds let a house state its whole style as data: its rules, the structure of its texts, its excuses and the judgments that stay with a reader.</p>
<!--main-->
<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>The grammar every declaration shares</h2>
    <p>A declaration is written <code>DEFINE KIND name ( FIELD value, ... )</code>. A keyword is UPPER_SNAKE, a name is lower_snake, a string stands between two double quotes on one line, and <code>//</code> opens a comment. The list of kinds is closed, and each one has a closed list of fields.</p>
    <p>A <code>USE "path.proz"</code> line reads another file, and <code>USE "prosa:reader_rights"</code> reads the constitution shipped with the language. A name is declared once across all the files read together.</p>
    <div class="scroll" role="region" aria-label="The ten kinds and their roles" tabindex="0"><table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">What it declares</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>STYLE</code></th><td>Groups rules</td><td>The voice of a house, or a set a house adopts</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>TERM</code></th><td>Rule</td><td>The house word for one thing, and the words refused in its place</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>WORDLIST</code></th><td>Rule</td><td>A list of refused words or phrases, with an optional budget</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>PATTERN</code></th><td>Rule</td><td>A refused construction, at a position when one is given</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>PROFILE</code></th><td>Rule</td><td>The measure of a voice, with bounds in both directions</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>FORM</code></th><td>Structure</td><td>The title, the first sentence, the headings and the close of a chapter, a page or a post</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>ZONE</code></th><td>Excuse</td><td>A region of a text excused from named codes, with a reason</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>ALLOW</code></th><td>Excuse</td><td>One line of one file excused from one code, with a reason</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>PIECE</code></th><td>Binding</td><td>A text bound to a style, a form and a subject</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>READING</code></th><td>Judgment of a reader</td><td>A question put to a reader, and the folder of its records</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>STYLE: a named set of rules</h2>
    <p>A <code>STYLE</code> holds rules, adopts other styles and declines the rules it leaves out. A rule binds a text only through a style: a rule that stands outside every style judges nothing. The public style of AYOUNI Press adopts the book style and holds the affirmative rule.</p>
    <!--proz: styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz ayouni_public-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>TERM: one word for one thing</h2>
    <p>A <code>TERM</code> names the house word and refuses the others. With <code>MENTIONS skipped</code>, a short quotation of the refused word is a mention, and the court leaves it unjudged.</p>
    <!--proz: styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz programmer-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>WORDLIST: a list of refused words</h2>
    <p>A <code>WORDLIST</code> refuses each entry of its list, and the field <code>SAYS</code> gives the sentence the diagnostic shows. A list with a budget, <code>AT_MOST 4 PER_THOUSAND</code>, is judged as one measure over the whole piece.</p>
    <!--proz: styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz hedges-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>PATTERN: a refused construction with its own test</h2>
    <p>A <code>PATTERN</code> is written over words: a row of words, a group in brackets, a gap with <code>WITHIN</code>, and a position with <code>AT</code>. Its <code>REFUSED</code> and <code>WRITTEN</code> examples are the test of the rule: the court refuses a pattern that accepts its refused example or refuses its written one.</p>
    <!--proz: styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz lack_listed-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>PROFILE: the measure of a voice</h2>
    <p>A <code>PROFILE</code> holds a text to bounds on its measures: the median sentence, the share of long and short sentences, and the marks per thousand words. Each measure outside its bound is one finding at level 4. The profile below belongs to the conformance suite.</p>
    <!--proz: conformance/fixtures/off-profile.proz voice-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>FORM: the structure of a page, a chapter or a post</h2>
    <p>A <code>FORM</code> places the title, the subject, the first sentence, the fixed headings and the close. The form <code>governed_text</code> belongs to the constitution, and the form <code>page</code> of the first house extends it with a title of six words at most.</p>
    <!--proz: constitution/reader_rights.proz governed_text-->
    <!--proz: styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz page-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>ZONE: a region excused with a reason</h2>
    <p>A <code>ZONE</code> excuses a region of a text from the codes it names. The region opens in the text at the comment <code>&lt;!-- prosa:zone record --&gt;</code> and closes at <code>&lt;!-- prosa:end --&gt;</code>.</p>
    <!--proz: conformance/fixtures/zone-lawful.proz record-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>ALLOW: one line excused with a reason</h2>
    <p>An <code>ALLOW</code> excuses one line of one file from one code. The excuse lives as long as the line: the court refuses, as <code>ALLOW_STALE</code>, an excuse that matches no finding.</p>
    <!--proz: conformance/fixtures/allow-lawful.proz quoted_record-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>PIECE: a text bound to its style</h2>
    <p>A <code>PIECE</code> names one file or a set of files, the style they follow, their form and their subject term. The piece below governs the pages of this site: each page states its subject in its head, and the court asks for that term in the title and in the first sentence.</p>
    <!--proz: site/site.proz site_pages-->
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>READING: a question that stays with a reader</h2>
    <p>A <code>READING</code> names a judgment the court leaves to a reader, a person or an AI model: it states the question and the folder of records. The command <code>prosa readings</code> lists each reading as recorded or due.</p>
    <!--proz: styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz voice_reader-->
  </div>
</section>

<section class="alt">
  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>The files the ten examples are cut from</h2>
    <p>Each example above is cut at build time from a file of the repository, so the page and the court read the same text.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The examples of <code>STYLE</code>, <code>TERM</code>, <code>WORDLIST</code>, <code>PATTERN</code>, <code>FORM</code> and <code>READING</code> come from <code>styles/ayouni/ayouni.proz</code>, the style of the first house.</li>
      <li>The form <code>governed_text</code> comes from <code>constitution/reader_rights.proz</code>.</li>
      <li>The examples of <code>PROFILE</code>, <code>ZONE</code> and <code>ALLOW</code> come from the conformance fixtures.</li>
      <li>The example of <code>PIECE</code> comes from <code>site/site.proz</code>, the declaration of this site.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
